const e={id:3,sections:[{id:"intro",type:"content",title:"Building Worlds from Detail",content:[{type:"text",value:"If the brain is a model-making machine, then the storyteller's job is to feed it the right raw material. In Chapter 3, Storr explores how **specific detail** transforms abstract settings into vivid, immersive worlds that readers inhabit."},{type:"callout",variant:"key-concept",value:"Great world-building isn't about describing everything. It's about selecting the *right* details — the ones that trigger the reader's brain to construct the rest of the world on its own."},{type:"text",value:'A single well-chosen detail can do more work than a paragraph of generic description. "The wallpaper was peeling at the seams, revealing older wallpaper beneath" tells us about age, neglect, and history in one image.'}]},{id:"specific-detail",type:"content",title:"The Power of Specific Detail",content:[{type:"text",value:`There's a hierarchy of descriptive power. At the bottom: abstractions. "It was a nice house." The brain gets almost nothing to work with. At the top: **specific, surprising detail** that triggers sensory simulation and reveals character simultaneously.`},{type:"example",title:"Detail Hierarchy",items:['"A house" — almost no neural simulation','"An old house" — slight improvement, still vague','"A Victorian house with a sagging porch" — getting somewhere','"A Victorian house where the porch had been patched with mismatched lumber, and a wind chime made of old silverware clinked in the doorway" — the brain builds a full world']},{type:"text",value:"The final example works because each detail is **specific** (mismatched lumber, silverware wind chime) and **suggestive** (someone resourceful lives here, someone with personality). The reader's brain fills in everything else."}]},{id:"quiz-1",type:"quiz",quizType:"formative",title:"Detail Selection",questions:[{id:"ch3-q1",type:"multiple-choice",conceptKey:"specific-detail",question:'Why does "a silverware wind chime" work better than "a decoration" in building a story world?',options:[{id:"a",text:"It uses more words",correct:!1},{id:"b",text:"It's more literary and impressive",correct:!1},{id:"c",text:"It gives the brain specific sensory data to simulate and implies character",correct:!0},{id:"d",text:"Readers prefer descriptions of silverware",correct:!1}],feedback:{correct:"Exactly! Specific detail does double duty: it triggers sensory simulation (you can hear the clinking) AND reveals character (someone creative/resourceful made this). Generic words trigger neither.",incorrect:"The power isn't in word count or impressiveness. It's that specific details give the brain's simulation machinery something **concrete** to work with, while also **implying** character and story."},hint:'What can your brain "see" and "hear" from each description?'}]},{id:"epistemic-trust",type:"content",title:"Earning the Reader's Trust",content:[{type:"text",value:"Specific detail doesn't just create immersion — it builds **epistemic trust**. When a writer demonstrates intimate knowledge of a world through precise detail, the reader trusts them as a guide. They surrender their skepticism and enter the story's reality."},{type:"text",value:"This is why research matters for fiction. A medical thriller that gets the surgery wrong loses trust. A historical novel with anachronistic details breaks the spell. The reader's brain is constantly checking: *does this person know what they're talking about?*"},{type:"callout",variant:"insight",value:"Epistemic trust is earned through specificity and lost through vagueness or inaccuracy. Once lost, it's nearly impossible to regain within a single story."}]},{id:"emotional-landscape",type:"content",title:"The World as Mindscape",content:[{type:"text",value:"One of Storr's most powerful insights: **the setting is not separate from the character**. How a character perceives and describes their world reveals their inner state. A depressed character sees a gray, oppressive city. A character in love sees magic in the same streets."},{type:"quote",value:"The landscape is the mindscape. The world the character sees is the world they carry inside them.",attribution:"Will Storr (paraphrased)"},{type:"text",value:"This means every description is doing characterization. When you describe a room through a character's eyes, you're not just building a setting — you're revealing who they are, what they notice, what they value, and what they fear."},{type:"tabs",tabs:[{label:"Anxious Character",content:'"The elevator doors closed with a final, mechanical sigh. The fluorescent light buzzed at a frequency that burrowed into her temples. She counted the floors — seven, eight, nine — each number a small prayer." The world is threatening, mechanical, counting-obsessed.'},{label:"Curious Character",content:'"The elevator doors closed, revealing a pattern of scratches on the inside panel — someone had been keeping a tally. She leaned closer. Thirty-seven marks. Who counts elevator rides?" The world is full of puzzles and mysteries.'},{label:"Grieving Character",content:`"The elevator doors closed. The building had been his building too, once. She could still see the smudge where he'd leaned his shoulder every morning, coffee in hand, humming something she could never identify." The world is saturated with absence.`}]}]},{id:"quiz-2",type:"quiz",quizType:"formative",title:"World as Character",questions:[{id:"ch3-q2",type:"scenario",conceptKey:"world-through-character",question:`You're writing a scene where a character walks through a park. Which approach best applies the "world through character" technique?`,options:[{id:"a",text:"Describe the park objectively: the trees, paths, and benches",correct:!1},{id:"b",text:"Filter every observation through what the character notices, values, or fears",correct:!0},{id:"c",text:"Use elaborate metaphors for each element of the park",correct:!1},{id:"d",text:"Keep description minimal and focus only on dialogue",correct:!1}],feedback:{correct:"Perfect! The key is subjectivity. A botanist notices different things than a fugitive. A child sees a different park than a widower. The world reveals the character.",incorrect:"The technique is about **filtering** the world through the character's subjective perception. What they notice (and ignore) reveals who they are."},hint:"Would a detective and a poet describe the same park the same way?"}]},{id:"exercise",type:"exercise",title:"World-Making Workshop",exercises:[{id:"ch3-ex1",type:"structured-template",xpReward:100,title:"Same Place, Different Eyes",instructions:"Describe the same location (a train station) through two different characters' perspectives. Each description should reveal character through what they notice.",fields:[{id:"character-a",label:"Character A: A recently released prisoner returning home",placeholder:"What does this character notice, feel, and think about in the station?"},{id:"character-b",label:"Character B: A child taking their first train ride",placeholder:"What does this character notice, feel, and think about in the station?"},{id:"reflection",label:"How did the choice of character change which details you selected?",placeholder:"Reflect on how perspective shaped your world-building..."}]}]},{id:"quiz-summative",type:"quiz",quizType:"summative",title:"Chapter 3 Assessment",passingScore:70,questions:[{id:"ch3-s1",type:"multiple-choice",conceptKey:"specific-detail",question:"According to the principle of specific detail, which description is most effective?",options:[{id:"a",text:'"The room was messy and uncomfortable"',correct:!1},{id:"b",text:'"Takeout containers covered every surface, their lids furry with mold"',correct:!0},{id:"c",text:'"The room had not been cleaned in a very long time"',correct:!1},{id:"d",text:`"It was a disgusting room that revealed the character's depression"`,correct:!1}],feedback:{correct:"Yes! Specific, concrete detail triggers neural simulation. The reader can SEE and almost SMELL the furry mold.",incorrect:"Effective description uses **specific, concrete** details that the brain can simulate — not abstractions or author commentary."}},{id:"ch3-s2",type:"multiple-choice",conceptKey:"epistemic-trust",question:'What is "epistemic trust" in storytelling?',options:[{id:"a",text:"The reader's trust that the story has a happy ending",correct:!1},{id:"b",text:"The reader's sense that the author knows what they're talking about",correct:!0},{id:"c",text:"Trust between characters within the story",correct:!1},{id:"d",text:"The author's trust in their own abilities",correct:!1}],feedback:{correct:"Correct! Epistemic trust is the reader's confidence in the author's authority. It's earned through precise, accurate detail.",incorrect:"Epistemic trust is about the **reader's confidence** that the author is a reliable guide to the story's world."}},{id:"ch3-s3",type:"multiple-choice",conceptKey:"emotional-landscape",question:'"The landscape is the mindscape" means:',options:[{id:"a",text:"Characters should only exist in natural settings",correct:!1},{id:"b",text:"Settings should always be described in exhaustive detail",correct:!1},{id:"c",text:"How a character perceives their environment reveals their inner psychological state",correct:!0},{id:"d",text:"Landscapes are more important than characters",correct:!1}],feedback:{correct:"Exactly! The world a character sees is filtered through their psychology. Description = characterization.",incorrect:"This principle means that **setting filtered through character perception** simultaneously builds world AND reveals inner state."}},{id:"ch3-s4",type:"fill-blank",conceptKey:"specific-detail",question:"Complete this principle of effective world-building:",sentence:"Great world-building isn't about describing _____. It's about selecting the _____ details.",blanks:[{id:"b1",answer:"everything",options:["everything","nothing","characters","emotions"]},{id:"b2",answer:"right",options:["right","most","longest","prettiest"]}],feedback:{correct:"Yes! You don't describe everything — you select the RIGHT details that trigger the reader's brain to construct the rest.",incorrect:"The key is: don't describe EVERYTHING, select the RIGHT details. Less but better."}},{id:"ch3-s5",type:"ordering",conceptKey:"detail-hierarchy",question:"Order these descriptions from LEAST to MOST effective:",items:[{id:"a",text:"A house"},{id:"b",text:"An old Victorian with mismatched lumber patches"},{id:"c",text:"A nice house"},{id:"d",text:"An old house"}],correctOrder:["a","c","d","b"],feedback:{correct:"Perfect! The hierarchy goes from vague abstractions to specific, suggestive detail.",incorrect:'The order is: "A house" (most vague) → "A nice house" (still abstract) → "An old house" (slight improvement) → the specific detail (most effective).'}}]},{id:"summary",type:"content",title:"Chapter Summary",content:[{type:"text",value:"## Key Takeaways from Chapter 3"},{type:"list",items:["**Specific detail > abstractions** for triggering neural simulation","**The right detail** does triple duty: setting + character + story","**Epistemic trust** is earned through precision and lost through vagueness","**The landscape is the mindscape** — description reveals character psychology","**World through character** — filter all description through subjective perception"]},{type:"callout",variant:"next",value:"**Next up**: Chapter 4 — The Domesticated Brain. Discover how theory of mind turns stories into empathy engines."}]}]};export{e as chapter3};
